    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Bryce Harper** (73.94) and **Taylor Ward** (71.46) represent our primary offensive engines. **Bryce Harper** remains an auto-start with his elite .400 xwOBA, catching a favorable matchup against Singer, while **Taylor Ward** benefits from a massive park factor boost (+16%) and a strong platoon advantage against the lefty Peterson, ensuring he occupies the prime #2 spot in the order. In the middle infield, both **Luis Arraez** (66.78) and **Geraldo Perdomo** (64.96) are elevated by their favorable matchups against Feltner and Canning, respectively. **Luis Arraez** is particularly well-positioned given the wind-out conditions at his venue, which should bolster his hit-tool efficiency. **Zack Gelof** (63.29) is a sneaky play; while facing a tough arm in Valdez, his ability to capitalize on the pitcher's slow delivery makes him a high-upside target for our scarce stolen base category.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision was benching **Masyn Winn** (38.41). While **Masyn Winn** typically provides stability, his score plummeted due to a difficult matchup against Henderson and an unfavorable park, falling just beneath our 40-point floor. Furthermore, a 38% rain risk introduces too much volatility for a player whose base projections were already stifled by the opponent's SP_xERA. Meanwhile, we continue to manage the absences of **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** and **Will Smith** on the IL, hoping that depth pieces like **Tyler Freeman** (64.87) can fill the void. **Tyler Freeman** draws an elite matchup against the struggling Whisenhunt (5.99 xERA), making him a surprisingly strong start to help stabilize our production in the absence of our injured core. We will keep a close eye on the weather and official lineups to ensure **Drew Gilbert** (52.93) and **JJ Bleday** (49.57) maintain their positions, as they remain in the \"marginal\" tier and carry more risk than our top-scoring starters.",
